==English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|loitren}}, from {{der|en|dum|loteren}} {{gl|"to shake, wag, wobble"; > modern {{cog|nl|leuteren|t=to dawdle, ramble}}}}, ultimately connected with a frequentative form of {{der|en|gem-pro|*lūtaną|t=to bend, stoop, cower, shrink from, decline}}, see {{l|en|lout}}. Cognate with {{cog|nl|leuteren||to dawdle}}, {{cog|gsw|lottern||to wobble}}, {{cog|de|Lotterbube||rascal}}. More at {{l|en|lout}}, {{l|en|little}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|en|/ˈlɔɪtə(ɹ)/}}
* {{a|US}} {{IPA|en|/ˈlɔɪtɚ/|[ˈlɔɪɾɚ]}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-loiter.ogg|Audio (US)}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɔɪtə(ɹ)|s=2}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# To stand about without any aim or purpose; to stand about [[idly]].
#: {{synonyms|en|lepak|q1=Malaysia, Singapore|linger|hang around}}
#: {{ux|en|For some reason, they discourage '''loitering''' outside the store, but encourage it inside.}}
# To remain at a certain place instead of moving on.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1834|author={{w|Letitia Elizabeth Landon}}|title=Francesca Carrara|volume=2|page=47|text=The dancing, which had been suspended, now recommenced with additional animation, and De Candale claimed Francesca's hand; but the rooms were crowded, and they stood for some time '''loitering''' on one of the terraces.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Daniel Taylor|authorlink=Daniel Taylor (journalist)|title=David Silva seizes point for [[w:Manchester City F.C.|Manchester City]] as [[w:Chelsea F.C.|Chelsea]] are checked|newspaper={{w|The Guardian}}|location=London|date=31 January 2015|url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2015/jan/31/chelsea-manchester-city-premier-league-match-report|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20170308125755/https://www.theguardian.com/football/2015/jan/31/chelsea-manchester-city-premier-league-match-report|archivedate=8 March 2017|passage=[[w:Sergio Agüero|[Sergio] Agüero]], as usual, was '''loitering''' with intent and swung his left foot at the ball. The shot was going wide but [[w:David Silva|[David] Silva]] was there to apply the decisive touch inside the six-yard area.}}
#* {{quote-journal|1=en|year=2019|author=Li Huang|author2=James Lambert|title=Another Arrow for the Quiver: A New Methodology for Multilingual Researchers|journal=Journal of Multilingual and Multicultural Development|doi=10.1080/01434632.2019.1596115|page=4|text=Using the transect method, the counter had to maintain a general progress along the transect and was not able to '''loiter''' at one spot for too long.}}
# {{lb|en|military|aviation}} For an aircraft to remain in the air near a target.

